WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
(light music)

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.790
- [Narrator] This satellite image

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:06.030
from the Port of Sevastopol
in Crimea on May 21

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.370
shows trucks lined up near a Russian ship

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.323
loading stolen Ukrainian grain.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:12.660
A Russian soldier

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.850
who drove one of the
trucks around this time

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.326
explained the details of
the smuggling operation

00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:20.915
- [Driver] Left our truck outside port.

00:00:20.915 --> 00:00:22.063
Other driver take in,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.597
put wheat on ship, finish.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.489
Two ships, two big ships.

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:31.168
No more, please.

00:00:31.168 --> 00:00:33.085
I cannot speak no more.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.240
- [Narrator] In may, the
U.S. State Department

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.980
sent cables to several countries

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.900
stating that three Russian ships,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.100
the Matros Pozynich, Matros
Koshka, and Mikhail Nenashev

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.190
were suspected of transporting
stolen Ukrainian grain.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.170
According to a senior European diplomat

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.670
who viewed the cable,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.700
Russian officials have
rejected such accusations,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.820
but we found these three ships
are part of a smuggling route

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.140
that Russia has been developing

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.790
to transport stolen grain

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.070
out of newly occupied areas of Ukraine

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.700
and into Russian allied
countries in the Middle East.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.760
We analyzed Telegram
posts, satellite imagery,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.500
and ship transponder data,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.660
reviewed documents and interviewed people

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.490
involved in the solicit trade

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.370
to reveal a 1500 mile smuggling route

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.560
that gives us a window
into the broader network

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.450
Russia has quietly assembled

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.990
to transport hundreds of
thousands of metric tons of grain

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.970
out of Ukraine.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.340
Russia has done this all
while obstructing Ukraine

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.890
from exporting its own grain and seeds,

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.590
up to 70 million metric tons per year,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.810
a large slice of the world's supply

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.750
much of which flows to
the developing world.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.020
In recent weeks, Ukraine said
Turkey detained a Russian ship

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.620
with stolen Ukrainian grain
while accusations of war crimes

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.473
and fears of a global
food crisis have mounted.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.393
The main root we uncovered
starts in Southeastern Ukraine.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.530
In the occupied City of
Kam'yanka-Dniprovs'ka

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.770
at one of the largest grain
storage facilities in the region

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.020
run by a company we aren't naming,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.810
we found evidence of dozens
of Russian-controlled trucks

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.873
transporting grain from this
facility since early May.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.470
The driver, we interviewed,
a soldier in the Russian army

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.570
who said he doesn't want
to fight in the war,

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.040
told us that in late May

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.390
a Russian Colonel ordered him

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.421
to pick up a load of
grain at this facility.

00:02:21.421 --> 00:02:24.260
- [Driver] With many trucks, 15 trucks.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.343
I had a white Kamaz truck

00:02:27.292 --> 00:02:28.983
and I come in the morning.

00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:32.733
May 21, 22, the colonel
put the trucks on line,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.313
we took wheat in, drive out.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.253
- [Narrator] Each truck

00:02:37.253 --> 00:02:39.720
can move around 10 to
50 metric tons of grain

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.340
in a single haul.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.660
In photos from the facility

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.320
that appear to be from early
May reviewed by the journal,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.320
we see trucks covered with
tarps, Zs on the front,

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.450
and concealed license plates.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.010
The Russian driver we interviewed

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.890
confirmed that he and the
other drivers in his caravan

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.333
drove similar trucks.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.720
Less than two kilometers from the depot,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.650
this photo posted to Facebook
on May 19 by a local resident

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.260
shows trucks with the
same characteristics;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.450
tarps and no license plates

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.673
headed away from the
grain storage facility.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.610
In satellite imagery from June 11,

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.000
we see similar vehicles
near the grain silos.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.890
Three days later, we
can see a line of them

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.840
outside the facility and a few more

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.283
in the parking lot as well.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.683
By June 22, there are over
30 of these trucks visible.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.700
The Russian driver said
his convoy of trucks

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.230
with stolen grain drove
Crimea on May 21 or 22.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.245
- [Driver] I hear on the
radio we go to Sevastopol.

00:03:43.245 --> 00:03:45.578
Many hours, but go same day.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.330
- [Narrator] We also found evidence

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.180
that another major grain storage facility

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:53.070
in the region was taken
over by the Russian military

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.800
seen here on March 26.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.290
A satellite image from
the same date and location

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.320
shows what appeared to
be those same tanks.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.130
This letter from an official
installed by Russian forces

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:08.163
informed the company that their
property was being seized.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.500
After the takeover, the company said

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.340
they were still able to track
their stolen trucks using GPS,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.210
which were loaded with grain
and also driven into Crimea.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.520
At one of the facilities
where grain was taken,

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.900
we can see that a Z,

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.530
a Russian pro-war symbol
has been marked on the roof.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.230
In addition to using truck
drivers from the Russian army,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.360
drivers for these routes are also hired

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.700
through messages posted to social media,

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.510
like this May 24th telegram
post by logistics operator

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.840
advertising nearly 20 truck
route to transport grain,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.900
mostly from newly occupied
Russian territory in Ukraine

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.100
to Crimea.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.190
This is a route similar to the
one our Russian driver took

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:53.553
and offers about $30 per ton in pay.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.480
Ukraine's access to
export route out of Crimea

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.170
has been largely cut off
since the 2014 annexation.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.840
Now, in videos published
over the last several weeks,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.870
we see long lines of trucks
heading south into the region.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:08.820
Many resemble the trucks
at the grain depot

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.490
in Kam'yanka-Dniprovs'ka
and fit the description

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.473
given by people involved in the trade.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.950
Once in Crimea, the Russian driver

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.590
said he and the other drivers
dropped off the trucks

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.380
at the Sevastopol Port
where many tons of grain

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.090
were unloaded to conveyor belts

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.780
and then moved to waiting ships

00:05:27.151 --> 00:05:29.220
- [Driver] Left our truck outside port.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.270
Other driver take in, put wheat on ships.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.920
- [Narrator] In satellite
imagery from Sevastopol

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.400
on May 19, 21, and 23,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.070
we can see three different
ships loading grain,

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:44.400
the same ones listed in
the State Department cable.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.720
The Matros Pozynich, Matros
Koshka, and Mikhail Nenashev

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.900
were all newly registered
in the months before the war

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.430
to a Russian state-owned company

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.040
called Crane Marine Contractor, LLC

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.980
according to a ship company database.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.320
Satellite images paired
with ship tracking data

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.870
from Marine traffic show these same ships

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.670
running regular roots from Crimea

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:09.240
to the Eastern Mediterranean
since at least late March,

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.160
often shutting off their transponders

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.870
as they approach ports,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.543
likely in an effort to
conceal their locations.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.960
For example, the Matros Pozynich

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.653
disappears from tracking
data near Istanbul on May 17.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.690
We then see it being loaded
with what looks like grain

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.183
at the port in Sevastopol two days later.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.430
The ships transponder
restarts again on May 21

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.860
after it departs Sevastopol.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:38.970
Over the next two days,

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.740
Marine traffic data shows it passes

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.540
through the Bosphorus Strait,

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.710
which we can see in photos
of the waterway from May 22.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.780
The ship's tracking is
then turned off again

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.370
as it nears its destination

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.010
with its last location reported on May 25

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.133
in the Mediterranean Sea
between Turkey and Cyprus.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.280
Soon after in Latakia, Syria,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.740
satellite imagery shows the ship docked

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.573
and its contents being unloaded.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.020
Commodity brokers involved in the network

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:08.190
say the grain was imported

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.353
by a Syrian state-run grain company.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.430
After similar journeys from Sevastopol

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.230
to the Eastern Mediterranean,

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.543
the Mikhail Nenashev docks in Turkey,

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.413
the Matros Koshka goes
dark for over a week,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.283
but reappears as it heads
back towards the Black Sea.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:29.550
Crane Marine contractor, LLC

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.410
and Syria's Ministry of Foreign Affairs

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.140
did not respond to requests for comment.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.060
The Russian embassy in Washington, DC

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.770
referred us to a prior statement

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.230
made by the Russian ambassador to the U.S.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.143
in which he rejected
accusations of grain theft.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.710
The frequency of thefts
at grain facilities

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.650
combined with the repetition
of these vessels' journeys,

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.590
and the sheer number of
trucks traveling into Crimea

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.300
shows that Russia is working quickly

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.033
to institutionalize its
grain smuggling network.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.390
In our last conversation
with the Russian truck driver

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.270
in late June, he said he was
waiting to receive orders

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.269
for his next truck route.

00:08:05.269 --> 00:08:08.390
- [Driver] I'm at the army
station at Sevastopol.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.040
I think I drive again for wheat.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:12.359
We wait.

00:08:12.359 --> 00:08:15.109
(tranquil music)

